{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Bezalel made the ark.</b> It is known that the ark was the most holy of all the vessels, for it is the dwelling-place of the Torah, and therefore in the making of the ark Scripture mentions Bezalel by name, for he was filled with the spirit of Elohim, in wisdom, in understanding, and in knowledge; and furthermore, the ark required intention, that it be made with its specific intention. As for the other vessels, where Scripture does not mention Bezalel at all but only says for each one ויעש — 'and he made' — without specification, it reverts to every wise-hearted man and to each of the craftsmen engaged in the work that Scripture mentions." "<b>And know</b> that these adjacent sections — ויעש בצלאל את הארון, and after it ויעש את השלחן, and after it ויעש את המנורה, and after it ויעש את מזבח הקטורת — were so arranged in the Tabernacle and in the Temple in this order: the ark in the innermost chamber, within the veil; the table outside the veil, on the north; the menorah on the south; and the incense altar was between the table and the menorah, drawn slightly outward, so that when the priest enters to trim the lamps he encounters it first. Hence they said: one removes the ashes of the incense altar before trimming the five lamps, because one does not pass over a commandment. And furthermore there is a hint in these four sections by their juxtaposition to the four letters of the Name: the height of the ark together with the cover corresponds to the יו״ד of the Name; the table, which is in the north, is a sign for the kingdom (malkhut) that receives influx from the fifth (sefirah), which is the left side that receives from the first ה, corresponding to the first ה of the Name — and therefore Scripture put the table before the menorah; the menorah, with its six branches, corresponds to the וא״ו of the Name; the incense altar, which is overlaid with gold, corresponds to the last ה of the Name, for incense draws from the attribute of judgment, as it says (Deuteronomy 33:10): ״ישימו קטורה באפך״." "<b>From this,</b> do not wonder at what they said: Bezalel knew how to combine the letters by which heaven and earth were created. For the יו״ד alluded to wisdom, and the ה from which emanation flows, and the וא״ו alluded to understanding, and the last ה is knowledge — and this is what is said: ואמלא אותו רוח אלהים בחכמה ובתבונה ובדעת." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> ויעש בצלאל את הארון. R. Hanina of Sepphoris said: Bezalel made three arks — two of gold and one of wood. He set the wooden one inside a golden one, and a golden one inside the wooden one, and overlaid its rim with gold, as it says (Exodus 25:11): מבית ומחוץ תצפנו. He said to him: Even though the wood is placed in the middle, treat it with honor, for the Torah rests within it. And the broken tablets — treat them with honor, as it says (Deuteronomy 10:2): אשר שברת ושמתם בארון — the tablets and the fragments of the tablets were placed in the ark. Even if you see children of Torah who are poor, treat them with honor. The ark was made of acacia-wood (עצי שטים) to atone for the sin of Shittim. When the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses to make the Tabernacle, for every single item He said to him ועשית — 'and you shall make,' but concerning the ark it is written ועשו — 'and they shall make,' so that no Israelite may open his mouth to say to his fellow: you have no share in the Torah. Therefore He commanded that all should bring. The Torah was given in the wilderness: just as the wilderness is free to all, so the words of Torah are free to all who wish — lest a person say: I am a child of Torah, and the Torah was given to me and to my fathers, while you and your fathers were not children of Torah but converts. Therefore it is written (Deuteronomy 33:4): תורה צוה לנו משה מורשה קהלת יעקב — to all who assemble in Jacob. Even those engaged in Torah who are converts are equivalent to the High Priest, as it says (Leviticus 18:5): אשר יעשה אותם האדם וחי בהם — it does not say priest or Levite or Israelite, but האדם. See what is written about the children of Jethro (1 Chronicles 2:55): ומשפחות סופרים יושבי יעבץ תרעתים שמעתים סוכתים — תרעתים: for they sat at the gates of the house of study; שמעתים: for they heard and learned; סוכתים: for they inclined their ears to hear with precise meaning, as in (Deuteronomy 27:9) הסכת ושמע. Another interpretation: תרעתים — for they heard the sounding (teru'ah) at Sinai; שמעתים — for they said נעשה ונשמע. And who are the Kenites? They descend from the family of the father of the house of Rechab, and they were the descendants of Jethro. And in tractate Yoma, at the end of the chapter 'Ba La,' it states: Bezalel made three arks — the inner one of gold, eight; the middle one of wood, nine; the outer one of gold, ten and a bit. What is 'a bit'? A rim."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {3 "Citations of 1 Chronicles 2:55: R. Bahya's verse label reads (דברי הימים א ב׳:נ״ה) — resolved as 1 Chronicles 2:55."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:07:50.082949121Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}